// Hypothesis (hunt / anomaly, not a fingerprint):
//   A Kerberos TGT issued for a USER account with RC4-HMAC (0x17) encryption is
//   worth reviewing in a domain that has standardised on AES. RC4 is one possible
//   by-product of overpass-the-hash (a stolen NTLM hash IS the RC4 key), but it is
//   NEITHER necessary (attackers can pass AES keys) NOR sufficient (legacy
//   accounts, trusts, and incomplete AES migrations all produce RC4 legitimately).
//   Treat a match as a signal to correlate, not as proof.
//
// Data source:
//   - Windows Security Event Log 4768 (Kerberos TGT / AS-REQ issued)
//
// Field notes:
//   - TicketEncryptionType: 0x17 = RC4-HMAC (legacy), 0x12 = AES256, 0x11 = AES128.
//   - Machine accounts ("$") and krbtgt are excluded to reduce known-benign RC4.
//   - This rule is only meaningful AFTER confirming the domain is AES-standardised;
//     otherwise it is high-noise. Baseline before enabling.

authentication where
  event.code == "4768" and
  winlog.event_data.TicketEncryptionType == "0x17" and
  not endsWith(winlog.event_data.TargetUserName, "$") and
  not winlog.event_data.TargetUserName in ("krbtgt", "-")
